Using the Euclidean algorithm, we find \[
\begin{aligned} \text{gcd}(10293, 29384) &= \text{gcd}(29384, 10293) \\ &= \text{gcd}(29384 - 2 \cdot 10293, 10293) \\ &= \text{gcd}(878, 10293) \\ &= \text{gcd}(10293 - 11 \cdot 878, 878) \\ &= \text{gcd}(235, 878) \\ &= \text{gcd}(878 - 3 \cdot 235, 235) \\ &= \text{gcd}(173, 235) \\ &= \text{gcd}(235 - 1 \cdot 173, 173) \\ &= \text{gcd}(62, 173) \\ &= \text{gcd}(173 - 2 \cdot 62, 62) \\ &= \text{gcd}(49, 62) \\ &= \text{gcd}(62 - 1 \cdot 49, 49) \\ &= \text{gcd}(13, 49) \\ &= \text{gcd}(49 - 3 \cdot 13, 13) \\ &= \text{gcd}(10, 13) \\ &= \text{gcd}(13 - 1 \cdot 10, 10) \\ &= \text{gcd}(3, 10) \\ &= \text{gcd}(10 - 3 \cdot 3, 3) \\ &= \text{gcd}(1, 3) \\ &= 1. \end{aligned}
\] Therefore, the greatest common divisor of 10293 and 29384 is $\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1

